    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d223">

                <pb facs="0343" n="263" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg263"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 Mutual Guarantee (Locarno)/495:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Dodd</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-03-21T13:00:00+01:00">March 21, 1936—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 21—11:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>88. Foreign Office attitude regarding the Locarno Powers London accord<anchor
                        n="16" xml:id="d223-f1"/> is generally negative. Foreign Office considers
                    that many of the proposals to Germany “smell Flandin” and are entirely
                    unacceptable, particularly the reestablishment of part of the demilitarized zone
                    on a one-sided basis and its occupation by foreign troops. The Foreign Office is
                    now studying the accord. While not yet decided on future moves it was clear that
                    the Germans have no desire or intention of breaking off the negotiations. We
                    gained the impression that there might well be counter proposals.</p>
                <p>In his speech last night Hitler followed the same general line of his previous
                    campaign speeches dealing largely with the international situation and asking,
                    at a high emotional climax, in which he referred <pb facs="0344" n="264"
                        xml:id="pg264"/> to how hard he has worked to build Germany up to her
                    present position, that the people should now in return show their gratitude in
                    the coming election. Hitler appeared to stress the idea that he would not give
                    way to any London action and of irritation with the attitude of the statesmen of
                    other powers.</p>
                <p>I am reliably informed that Ribbentrop is returning from London this afternoon
                    presumably for a conference with Hitler.</p>
                <p>Repeated to London, Paris, Geneva, Rome.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dodd</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
